  1. Antioxidants and Exercise Performance: With a Focus on Vitamin E and C Supplementation
     Madalyn Riley Higgins, Azimeh Izadi, Mojtaba Kaviani
     PMC7697466 · International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health · 2020-11-15
     https://doi.org/10.3390/ijerph17228452
     "This paper reviews antioxidant supplementation, specifically vitamins E and C, for exercise performance. Evidence suggests supplementation may hinder exercise adaptations and is generally not beneficial, except in specific circumstances."

  2. Does Vitamin C and E Supplementation Impair the Favorable Adaptations of Regular Exercise?
     Michalis G. Nikolaidis, Chad M. Kerksick, Manfred Lamprecht, Steven R. McAnulty
     PMC3425865 · Oxidative Medicine and Cellular Longevity · 2012-01-01
     https://doi.org/10.1155/2012/707941
     "This study investigated whether vitamin C and E supplementation interferes with exercise adaptations. High doses of these vitamins are not recommended for exercising individuals due to conflicting evidence on their efficacy and potential harm."

  3. Effects of vitamin C and E supplementation on endogenous antioxidant systems and heat shock proteins in response to endurance training
     Kristoffer T. Cumming, Truls Raastad, Geir Holden, Nasser E. Bastani, Damaris Schneeberger, Maria Pa...
     PMC4254089 · Physiological Reports · 2014-10-01
     https://doi.org/10.14814/phy2.12142
     "This study examined vitamin C and E supplementation's effects on antioxidant and heat shock protein systems during endurance training. Supplementation did not alter long-term training adaptations but increased acute exercise stress responses."

  4. Can supplementation with vitamin C and E alter physiological adaptations to strength training?
     Gøran Paulsen, Kristoffer T Cumming, Håvard Hamarsland, Elisabet Børsheim, Sveinung Berntsen, Truls ...
     PMC4114441 · BMC Sports Science, Medicine and Rehabilitation · 2014-01-01
     https://doi.org/10.1186/2052-1847-6-28
     "High doses of vitamins C and E were tested for their effects on strength training adaptations in young and older men. Supplementation blunted adaptations in young men but showed potential benefits in older men."

  5. Effect of high-dose vitamin C and E supplementation on muscle recovery and training adaptation: a mini review
     Jooyoung Kim
     PMC10440181 · Physical Activity and Nutrition · 2023-06-01
     https://doi.org/10.20463/pan.2023.0012
     "This review examined high-dose vitamin C and E supplementation's effects on exercise-induced muscle recovery and training adaptation. Evidence suggests potential negative impacts on adaptation, with unclear benefits for recovery."

  6. Vitamin C and E supplementation does not affect heat shock proteins or endogenous antioxidants in trained skeletal muscles during 12 weeks of strength training
     K. T. Cumming, T. Raastad, A. Sørstrøm, M. P. Paronetto, N. Mercatelli, I. Ugelstad, D. Caporossi, G...
     PMC7050865 · BMC nutrition · 2017-08-17
     https://doi.org/10.1186/s40795-017-0185-8
     "This study examined the impact of vitamin C and E supplementation on trained individuals undergoing strength training. The supplementation did not alter acute exercise stress responses or long-term adaptations in heat shock proteins or endogenous antioxidants."

